{"tool_name":"beast2","contents":["biocontainers","galaxy","bioschemas","biotools","oeb"],"fetched_metadata":{"biocontainers":{"name":"beast2","identifiers":["biotools:beast2","doi:10.1371/JOURNAL.PCBI.1003537"],"license":"LGPL (>= 2.1)","summary":"Beast 2 is a cross-platform program for bayesian phylogenetic analysis of molecular sequences.","home_url":"https://github.com/CompEvol/beast2","keywords":["Bayes Theorem","Biological Evolution","Programming Languages","Phylogenetics","Phylogenomics","Genetics"],"manually_check":true,"total_pulls":1},"biotools":{"id":"beast2","home":"http://www.beast2.org","summary":"Bayesian phylogenetic analysis of molecular sequences. It estimates rooted, time-measured phylogenies using strict or relaxed molecular clock models. It can be used as a method of reconstructing phylogenies but is also a framework for testing evolutionary hypotheses without conditioning on a single tree topology. It uses Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) to average over tree space, so that each tree is weighted proportional to its posterior probability. It includes a graphical user-interface for setting up standard analyses and a suit of programs for analysing the results.","addition_date":"2018-10-15T17:35:28Z","last_update_date":"2018-12-10T12:59:01Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Desktop application"],"version":["2.5.1"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:beast2","credit":[{"typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"],"url":"http://www.beast2.org"}],"documentation":[{"type":["Training material"],"url":"http://www.beast2.org/tutorials/"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Phylogenetic tree analysis (shape)","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0551"},{"term":"Phylogenetic tree reconstruction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3478"}]}],"name":"BEAST 2","owner":"aotamendi.1","publication":[{"doi":"10.1371/JOURNAL.PCBI.1003537","metadata":{"abstract":"We present a new open source, extensible and flexible software platform for Bayesian evolutionary analysis called BEAST 2. This software platform is a re-design of the popular BEAST 1 platform to correct structural deficiencies that became evident as the BEAST 1 software evolved. Key among those deficiencies was the lack of post-deployment extensibility. BEAST 2 now has a fully developed package management system that allows third party developers to write additional functionality that can be directly installed to the BEAST 2 analysis platform via a package manager without requiring a new software release of the platform. This package architecture is showcased with a number of recently published new models encompassing birth-death-sampling tree priors, phylodynamics and model averaging for substitution models and site partitioning. A second major improvement is the ability to read/write the entire state of the MCMC chain to/from disk allowing it to be easily shared between multiple instances of the BEAST software. This facilitates checkpointing and better support for multi-processor and high-end computing extensions. Finally, the functionality in new packages can be easily added to the user interface (BEAUti 2) by a simple XML template-based mechanism because BEAST 2 has been re-designed to provide greater integration between the analysis engine and the user interface so that, for example BEAST and BEAUti use exactly the same XML file format. \u00a9 2014 Bouckaert et al.","authors":[{"name":"Bouckaert R."},{"name":"Drummond A.J."},{"name":"Heled J."},{"name":"Kuhnert D."},{"name":"Rambaut A."},{"name":"Suchard M.A."},{"name":"Vaughan T."},{"name":"Wu C.-H."},{"name":"Xie D."}],"citationCount":3251,"date":"2014-01-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"PLoS Computational Biology","title":"BEAST 2: A Software Platform for Bayesian Evolutionary Analysis"},"pmcid":"PMC3985171","pmid":"24722319"}],"topic":[{"term":"Phylogenetics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3293"},{"term":"Phylogenomics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0194"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"BEAST 2","home":"https://bio.tools/beast2","version":"2.5.1","summary":"Bayesian phylogenetic analysis of molecular sequences. It estimates rooted, time-measured phylogenies using strict or relaxed molecular clock models. It can be used as a method of reconstructing phylogenies but is also a framework for testing evolutionary hypotheses without conditioning on a single tree topology. It uses Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) to average over tree space, so that each tree is weighted proportional to its posterior probability. It includes a graphical user-interface for setting up standard analyses and a suit of programs for analysing the results.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"galaxy":{"version":"2.7.7","first_commit":"2026-07-20","conda_name":"beast2","conda_version":"2.7.7","summary":"BEAST 2: Bayesian Evolutionary Analysis Sampling Trees","edam_operations":["Phylogenetic tree reconstruction","Phylogenetic tree analysis (shape)"],"edam_topics":["Phylogenetics","Phylogenomics"],"toolshed_categories":["Phylogenetics"],"toolshed_id":"beast2","users_5_years":0,"users_all_time":0,"usage_5_years":0,"usage_all_time":0,"bio_tools_summary":"Bayesian phylogenetic analysis of molecular sequences. It estimates rooted, time-measured phylogenies using strict or relaxed molecular clock models. It can be used as a method of reconstructing phylogenies but is also a framework for testing evolutionary hypotheses without conditioning on a single tree topology. It uses Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) to average over tree space, so that each tree is weighted proportional to its posterior probability. It includes a graphical user-interface for setting up standard analyses and a suit of programs for analysing the results.","bio_tools_ids":"beast2","bio_tools_name":"BEAST 2","related_tutorials":[],"related_workflows":[],"tool_ids":["beast2_beast","beast2_logcombiner","beast2_treeannotator"],"tool_output_formats":["txt","tabular","beast.trees"],"hom